(   [Problem Statement]: Explain the molecular mechanism of action of five major translation-targeting antibiotics \(Streptomycin, Tetracycline, Chloramphenicol, Erythromycin, Puromycin\).) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Mechanism of Translation-Targeting Antibiotics:) Tj
T*
(   1. Streptomycin \(Aminoglycoside\):) Tj
T*
(   - Binds to the 16S rRNA decoding region of the 30S subunit \(near protein S12\).) Tj
T*
(   - Distorts the A-site conformation, inducing misreading of the genetic code and generating) Tj
T*
(   aberrant, toxic proteins. At high concentrations, completely inhibits initiation complex) Tj
T*
(   assembly.) Tj
T*
(   2. Tetracycline:) Tj
T*
(   - Reversibly binds to the 30S subunit, physically obstructing the accommodation of) Tj
T*
(   aminoacyl-tRNA into the ribosomal A-site, halting elongation.) Tj
T*
(   3. Chloramphenicol:) Tj
T*
(   - Binds directly to the peptidyl transferase cavity of the 23S rRNA in the 50S subunit.) Tj
T*
(   - Sterically inhibits peptide bond formation by blocking the positioning of the aminoacyl) Tj
T*
(   moiety of A-site tRNA.) Tj
T*
(   4. Erythromycin \(Macrolide\):) Tj
T*
(   - Binds to the 23S rRNA lining the polypeptide exit tunnel within the 50S subunit near the PTC.) Tj
T*
(   - Does not inhibit peptide bond formation directly, but sterically blocks the transit of) Tj
T*
(   nascent polypeptide chains, causing peptidyl-tRNA drop-off after 4-8 amino acids are) Tj
T*
(   polymerized.) Tj
T*
(   5. Puromycin \(Aminonucleoside\):) Tj
T*
(   - Acts as a structural mimic of the 3' end of tyrosyl-tRNA.) Tj
T*
(   - Enters the ribosomal A-site and accepts the nascent polypeptide chain from the P-site via a) Tj
T*
(   stable amide bond. Because it lacks a tRNA body, the puromycin-peptide conjugate dissociates) Tj
T*
(   prematurely, terminating translation.) Tj
T*
