(   - Five structural genes \(trpE, trpD, trpC, trpB, trpA\) encode enzymes for tryptophan) Tj
T*
(   biosynthesis.) Tj
T*
(   - Controlled by promoter Ptrp, operator Otrp, and a 162-nt leader region \(trpL\) containing an) Tj
T*
(   attenuator.) Tj
T*
(   2. Trp Repressor-Mediated Negative Control:) Tj
T*
(   - The trpR gene encodes an apo-repressor that cannot bind DNA alone.) Tj
T*
(   - When tryptophan levels are abundant: Two tryptophan molecules bind the apo-repressor as) Tj
T*
(   corepressors. This induces a conformational reorientation of its helix-turn-helix motifs,) Tj
T*
(   allowing it to bind Otrp and block RNA polymerase binding \(~70-fold repression\).) Tj
T*
(   3. Attenuation Mechanism \(Fine-Tuning Control\):) Tj
T*
(   - Operates through tight coupling of transcription and translation within trpL.) Tj
T*
(   - The trpL transcript encodes a 14-amino-acid leader peptide containing two consecutive) Tj
T*
(   tryptophan codons \(Trp-Trp\) and four RNA segments \(1, 2, 3, 4\) capable of forming mutually) Tj
T*
(   exclusive hairpins.) Tj
T*
(   - High Tryptophan: The translating ribosome rapidly traverses the Trp codons and covers segment) Tj
T*
(   2. This leaves segment 3 free to pair with segment 4, forming a stable 3:4 GC-rich hairpin) Tj
T*
(   followed by a poly-U tract \(intrinsic Rho-independent terminator\). RNA polymerase terminates) Tj
T*
(   transcription before reaching trpE.) Tj
T*
(   - Low Tryptophan: The ribosome stalls at the tandem Trp codons on segment 1 due to lack of) Tj
T*
(   Trp-tRNA^Trp. Segment 2 remains free to base-pair with segment 3, forming a 2:3 antiterminator) Tj
T*
(   hairpin. This prevents formation of the 3:4 terminator, allowing RNA polymerase to transcribe) Tj
T*
(   the entire structural operon.) Tj
