/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Explain Co-translational and Post-translational Protein Translocation into the Endoplasmic Reticulum via the Signal Recognition Particle \(SRP\) and Sec61 translocon.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Protein Translocation Across the Endoplasmic Reticulum:) Tj
T*
(   1. Signal Peptide Architecture:) Tj
T*
(   - Proteins targeted to the secretory pathway, lysosomes, or plasma membrane contain an) Tj
T*
(   N-terminal Signal Peptide \(15-30 amino acids\) comprising a positively charged N-region, a) Tj
T*
(   hydrophobic core \(h-region, 6-15 residues\), and a polar cleavage site recognized by signal) Tj
T*
(   peptidase.) Tj
T*
(   2. Signal Recognition Particle \(SRP\) Targeting:) Tj
T*
(   - As the signal peptide emerges from the ribosomal polypeptide exit tunnel, it is recognized) Tj
T*
(   and bound by SRP \(a cytosolic ribonucleoprotein complex consisting of 7S RNA and six proteins:) Tj
T*
(   SRP9, SRP14, SRP19, SRP54, SRP68, SRP72\).) Tj
T*
(   - SRP54 binds the hydrophobic signal sequence, while the Alu domain \(SRP9/14\) arrests) Tj
T*
(   translation elongation to prevent premature folding in the cytosol.) Tj
T*
(   3. ER Docking and Translocon Engagement:) Tj
T*
(   - The ribosome-nascent chain-SRP complex docks at the rough ER membrane by binding the SRP) Tj
T*
(   Receptor \(SRalpha/SRbeta heterodimer\).) Tj
T*
(   - Both SRP54 and SRalpha are GTPases. Reciprocal GTP binding stabilizes the docking complex;) Tj
T*
ET

